---
// About: who makes this and how. Written 2026-10-01 after Google's 09-28
// demotion, approved by Hidde in session ("moeten we niet open zijn dat we
// starten met officiele register en ai - maar dat we de gebruikers vragen om
// ons te helpen? en dat we contact opnemen met de lokale partijen?").
//
// Why it exists: Google's own self-assessment asks "Is it self-evident to your
// visitors who authored your content?" and "Is the use of automation,
// including AI-generation, self-evident to visitors through disclosures?", and
// this site answered neither anywhere. So it says plainly where the trees come
// from, that the stories are drafted with AI from the sources each tree page
// lists, and where readers and local organisations come in.
//
// Convention: AllTrails, komoot and iNaturalist all carry an About page linked
// from the footer. No personal name: it went in on 2026-10-01 for Google's
// "who made this" question and came out the same day (Hidde: "als die about
// pagina niks doet haal mn naam dan maar weer weg"), since Google says bylines
// do not help ranking. No email address either, per the v1.4 privacy ruling.
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import { breadcrumbSchema, ldScript, SITE_SCHEMA, BASE_URL } from "../lib/schema";
import { submitLink } from "../lib/tree-copy";

const canonical = `${BASE_URL}/about`;
const crumbItems: [string, string | null][] = [["Home", BASE_URL], ["About", null]];
const description = "Where our trees come from, how each one is checked, how the stories are written, and how readers and local tree groups correct us.";

const graph = [
  ...SITE_SCHEMA,
  breadcrumbSchema(crumbItems, canonical),
  {
    "@type": "AboutPage",
    name: "About Ancient Trees",
    description,
    url: canonical,
  },
];
---
<Base title="About Ancient Trees" description={description} canonical={canonical} headExtra={ldScript(graph)}>
<main class="content-page">
  <Breadcrumbs items={crumbItems} />
  <h1>About Ancient Trees</h1>
  <p class="lede">Somewhere near you stands a tree that was already old when the street around it was built. Ancient Trees shows you where it is, why it is worth the walk and how to get to the trunk. It is an independent project from the Netherlands.</p>
  <div class="prose-block">
    <h2>Where the trees come from</h2>
    <p>Most of our trees come from official registers: the lists of protected and monumental trees that governments and city councils publish. Others come from council tree walks, tree societies, local newspapers and readers who send us a tree. From all of these we pick the ones worth an afternoon out. We leave out trees that have died, trees on private land, and trees whose location is kept secret to protect them.</p>

    <h2>How the stories are written</h2>
    <p>We draft the stories with AI, using the sources listed on each tree page. That means we sometimes get something wrong. If you spot a mistake, tell us and we will fix it.</p>

    <h2>Ambassadors</h2>
    <p>A city can have an ambassador: someone who knows its old trees and helps us keep the list right. Ambassadors tell us when a tree has fallen, point out trees we missed and add photographs. If you know the old trees of your city, you can offer to become its ambassador on the city page.</p>

    <h2>How you can help</h2>
    <p>When you stand in front of one of these trees, you know things we do not: whether it is still standing, which trunk it is, how big it really is. Every tree page has a button to tell us, or to add your photograph. You can also <a href={submitLink("tree")}>send us a tree we are missing</a>.</p>

    <h2>Working with the people who care for the trees</h2>
    <p>We write to park services, botanical gardens, tree registers and local tree groups. Several of them have already corrected or confirmed trees we map, and their corrections are on the map.</p>

    <p>Where our facts come from, register by register, is on the <a href="/sources">sources page</a>.</p>
  </div>
</main>
</Base>
